> From orientation to a supporting edge

For origin a, incumbent b and examined point q, the predicate is the sign of

$$
D(a,b,q)=(b_x-a_x)(q_y-a_y)-(b_y-a_y)(q_x-a_x).
$$
\alttext(The determinant is the horizontal displacement to b times vertical displacement to q, minus vertical displacement to b times horizontal displacement to q; its sign identifies the turn.)

A negative sign replaces b with q. A zero sign keeps the farther point. At a
hull vertex, collinear alternatives lie on the same ray: opposite rays would put
the vertex inside their segment. Lexicographic sorted order is monotone along
that ray, so the implementation selects the farther sorted index without
squaring a distance. The predicate returns an exact sign of the represented
finite binary64 coordinates; it does not interpret noisy measurements with a
tolerance. Subtraction and multiplication in ordinary double arithmetic would
not give this guarantee.

Each completed scan leaves every point on or to the left of the selected
directed edge. Starting at the lexicographic minimum, this supporting-edge
invariant advances counterclockwise through strict extreme points until
returning to the start. Farthest selection excludes intermediate collinear
points. The returned hull is unclosed; an all-collinear cloud returns its two
extremes.

> Retain the original twenty points

The fixture retains all original coordinates and input order. The figure uses
equal coordinate scales and draws the actual result without recomputing another
hull. The table exposes the earliest original row for each selected vertex.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jarvis_cloud.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/jarvis_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <limits>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto points = dm::geometry::jarvis_demo_points();
    const auto a = dm::geometry::jarvis_march_analyze(points);
    std::print("| vertex | original row | x | y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.hull.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, a.input_indices[i],
                   a.hull[i].x, a.hull[i].y);
    std::print("\n| input rows | distinct points | hull vertices | orientations | sort comparisons | " "duplicate checks |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | " "{} |\n",
               points.size(), a.work.distinct_points, a.hull.size(),
               a.work.orientation_tests, a.work.sort_comparisons,
               a.work.duplicate_comparisons);
    if (a.hull.size() != 8 || a.work.orientation_tests != 144)
        throw std::runtime_error("original fixture");
    dm::geometry::jarvis_hull_plot(points, a).title("Original twenty points: eight hull vertices").save_svg("build/jarvis_cloud.svg");
}
