> Count the scan, then account for preparation

With u distinct points and h hull vertices, each edge tests every point except
its origin and initial seed. For u at least three, the actual orientation count
is h(u−2), with h starts and h selections: hu trace events. The small-input
shortcuts are separate. The complete implementation sorts n original records and
removes adjacent duplicates, so its bound is O(n log n + uh), with O(n)
preparation storage. Calling the wrapper output-sensitive describes its wrapping
phase; a small hull does not remove sorting overhead. Trace storage adds O(uh).
Counts are logical operations, not timings or byte-level memory traffic.

The four-corner family adds strict interior points. The second family uses
(i,i²); every point is extreme because its tangent y=2ix−i² leaves every other
point strictly above by (j−i)². These controls isolate h=4 from h=u without
random sampling. Plot coordinates are explicitly base-two logarithms of the
measured counts.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jarvis_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/jarvis_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <limits>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, small, full;
    std::print("| distinct u | h=4 tests | h=u tests | box sort | parabola sort |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n = 8; n <= 256; n *= 2) {
        const auto a =
            dm::geometry::jarvis_march_analyze(dm::geometry::jarvis_control_points(n));
        const auto b =
            dm::geometry::jarvis_march_analyze(dm::geometry::jarvis_control_points(n, true));
        if (a.work.orientation_tests != 4 * (n - 2) ||
            b.work.orientation_tests != n * (n - 2))
            throw std::runtime_error("work identity");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.work.orientation_tests,
                   b.work.orientation_tests, a.work.sort_comparisons,
                   b.work.sort_comparisons);
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        small.push_back(std::log2(a.work.orientation_tests));
        full.push_back(std::log2(b.work.orientation_tests));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, small, "h=4", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, small, "",
                                                     {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(
        x, full, "h=u", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, full, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4).title("Actual wrapping work as the hull grows").x_label("log2(distinct points)").y_label("log2(orientation tests)").y_limits(4, 18).save_svg("build/jarvis_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| distinct u | h=4 tests | h=u tests | box sort | parabola sort |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 24 | 48 | 24 | 14 |
| 16 | 56 | 224 | 56 | 30 |
| 32 | 120 | 960 | 166 | 101 |
| 64 | 248 | 3968 | 557 | 307 |
| 128 | 504 | 16128 | 1718 | 797 |
| 256 | 1016 | 65024 | 4620 | 1967 |
\image(figures/jarvis_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual wrapping work for fixed and growing hull sizes.)
\alttext(Logarithmic curves separate approximately linear orientation counts for four hull vertices from quadratic counts when all points are extreme.)

> Ownership, limits and evidence

Analyze and trace borrow input during execution and return owned results.
Default limits admit one million original points and one million actual events;
zero disables the corresponding optional ceiling. Complete point admission
precedes indexed allocation, while each event is admitted before its operation.
Failure throws rather than returning a partial history. Untraced analysis
ignores the event limit. These ceilings count records, not allocation capacity
or peak RAM. The legacy by-value interface copies its argument at the call
boundary; its signed counter changes only after successful completion and
checked addition.

Fourteen targeted native tests cover ordinary and degenerate hulls, identities,
actual state transitions and guards. An independent rational oracle checks 1,834
clouds, 251,230 actual events and 231,412 orientation tests, including 300
full-exponent finite-coordinate clouds. A frozen legacy comparison preserves
hulls, counts and 1,293,757 scan operand triples on 5,001 ordinary inputs, while
documenting the subnormal-line correction. These checks establish the tested
contracts rather than a performance comparison with other hull algorithms. No
external dataset is needed for these geometric examples.

> Reference

R. A. Jarvis (1973), “On the identification of the convex hull of a finite set
of points in the plane”, Information Processing Letters 2(1), 18–21,
[doi:10.1016/0020-0190(73)90020-3|https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(73)90020-3].
This report uses sorted deduplication and exact represented-coordinate signs;
its preparation and counted scan are specified above.
